# ============================================================
# .gitignore for IITJ-Sem2-AI-DS
# Python | Jupyter Notebook | VS Code | Windows
# ============================================================


# ------------------------------------------------------------
# JUPYTER NOTEBOOK CHECKPOINTS
# ------------------------------------------------------------
# Every time you save a .ipynb file, Jupyter automatically
# creates a backup copy inside a hidden folder called
# .ipynb_checkpoints/. You never need this in Git — it's just
# Jupyter's internal autosave. Without this line, every repo
# push would include these junk copies.
.ipynb_checkpoints/
*/.ipynb_checkpoints/


# ------------------------------------------------------------
# PYTHON CACHE FILES
# ------------------------------------------------------------
# When Python runs a .py file, it compiles it to bytecode and
# stores it in __pycache__/ as .pyc files. This speeds up
# future runs. But they're machine-generated and machine-
# specific — there's zero reason to commit them.
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*$py.class


# ------------------------------------------------------------
# VIRTUAL ENVIRONMENTS
# ------------------------------------------------------------
# If you ever create a virtual environment (venv) to isolate
# your Python packages, the folder can be 100MB+. Never commit
# it. Instead, commit a requirements.txt file so others can
# recreate it. The names below are the most common venv folder
# names people use.
venv/
env/
.env/
.venv/
ai_env/
.idea



# ------------------------------------------------------------
# VS CODE SETTINGS
# ------------------------------------------------------------
# VS Code stores your personal editor settings (theme, font
# size, keybindings, extensions) in a .vscode/ folder. These
# are YOUR preferences, not part of the project. Committing
# them forces your settings on everyone else who clones the
# repo — including future you on a different machine.
#
# EXCEPTION: If you want to share specific settings with
# collaborators (like recommended extensions), you can
# un-ignore specific files, e.g.:
#   !.vscode/extensions.json
.vscode/


# ------------------------------------------------------------
# LARGE DATA FILES
# ------------------------------------------------------------
# Datasets, especially CSVs and Excel files, can be huge.
# Git is not designed to store large binary or data files —
# it slows down the repo significantly. If your dataset is
# small (under ~5MB), it's fine to commit. But it's good
# practice to ignore common data formats by default and only
# explicitly add small ones when needed with:
#   git add -f data/small_file.csv
#
# Note: You already have a dataset/ folder in Pattern
# Recognition — check its size before your next push.

# *.csv
# *.xlsx
# *.xls
*.parquet
*.h5
*.hdf5
*.pkl
*.pickle


# ------------------------------------------------------------
# IMAGES AND OUTPUTS (generated ones)
# ------------------------------------------------------------
# Matplotlib and seaborn plots saved as files, or any
# auto-generated output images. If you intentionally want
# to commit a specific visualization, use:
#   git add -f my_plot.png
# But don't let generated outputs pile up automatically.
# *.png    ← commented out because you may want to commit
# *.jpg       diagrams or reference images intentionally.
#             Uncomment if you find images cluttering your repo.


# ------------------------------------------------------------
# ENVIRONMENT VARIABLE FILES
# ------------------------------------------------------------
# If you ever use API keys, passwords, or secrets (e.g., for
# a future project using OpenAI API, cloud services, etc.),
# they go in a .env file. This line ensures you NEVER
# accidentally push secrets to a public GitHub repo.
# This is a critical security habit to build early.
.env
*.env


# ------------------------------------------------------------
# OS-GENERATED FILES
# ------------------------------------------------------------
# Windows creates Thumbs.db (thumbnail cache) and desktop.ini
# in every folder. macOS creates .DS_Store files. These are
# completely irrelevant to your code and should never appear
# in a repo.
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini
.DS_Store


# ------------------------------------------------------------
# JUPYTER NOTEBOOK OUTPUT STRIPPING (optional but recommended)
# ------------------------------------------------------------
# This doesn't go in .gitignore, but worth knowing:
# Notebook outputs (graphs, print statements, model outputs)
# are saved inside the .ipynb file itself as JSON. This means
# every time you re-run a cell, Git sees the entire notebook
# as "changed" even if your code didn't change.
# 
# To fix this, consider using `nbstripout` — a tool that
# automatically strips outputs before committing:
#   pip install nbstripout
#   nbstripout --install   (run once in your repo folder)
#
# This keeps your diffs clean and your commits meaningful.

